text:
To M. P.Goodfellow

By Kiusic Kimm (May 22, 1916)

It was in the merry month of may,
Nature full of joy all hearts did sway;
After counting seven, eight and nine,
When the weather was so nice and fine,
There arrived a Goodfellow by name;
Destined was he as a man of fame
Trots he mom till dusk both East and West,
Always trying what he thinks'is best,

Sometime when we're tired and feeling blue,
Comes he as a friend well tried and true,
Word of Ch er and hope he has for all,
Ever holding up one who might fall,
He may never bellow, bray or brag,
But for man to wave his freedom's flag;
Claim we him a friend of ours to be
'Cause he wants to see us really free;

Hope he'll soon be back amidst our cheer
as the first amba    sador to here;
Then we'll all sit by the old plum tree;
In a joyful mood and real good spree;
Come you quick before it's late and dank,
Help 0ur down-drooped hearts to soar like lark;
Bon voyage to you and God speed back,
And may life leave nothing you may lack!
